One failed request shouldn't blank your whole dashboard. Promise.all() does exactly that.

Promise.all() rejects immediately when any input rejects, with the first reason. The other operations aren't cancelled. They keep running and their results are thrown away (MDN).

Promise.allSettled() waits for every promise and gives you one object each, in input order:

const results = await Promise.allSettled([getUser(), getOrders()]);
// { status: "fulfilled", value } or { status: "rejected", reason }

My rule of thumb: Promise.all when the tasks depend on each other and a partial result is useless. Promise.allSettled when they're independent and you can render what succeeded. One catch: results come back by position, so don't mix up the order when you destructure.

Where do you still reach for Promise.all, and where did allSettled replace it?
